  "articles": [
    {
      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "The continuous or 'ing' tense in Hindi is formed by adding the suffixes 'रहा', 'रहे' or 'रही' to the verb root. The form agrees with the gender and number of the subject: 'रहा' for masculine singular, 'रहे' for masculine plural and mixed groups, and 'रही' for feminine subjects. For example, 'मैं जा रहा हूँ' means 'I am going' (masculine speaker), 'सब आ रहे हैं' means 'They are all coming' (masculine subject), and 'बस पहुँच रही है' means 'The bus is arriving'.\nIn negative continuous sentences, 'नहीं' is usually placed before the root. The auxiliary verb may be omitted, and in feminine plural forms the nasalization moves from the omitted 'हैं' onto 'रही', as in 'गाड़ियाँ नहीं पहुँच रही' which means 'The trains aren't arriving'.\nThe expressions 'कब से' (since when?) and 'अब तक' (until now) are used to ask about the duration of an action. For example, 'यहाँ कब से रह रही है?' asks 'How long have you been living here?' and 'क्या वह अब तक सो रही है?' means 'Is she still sleeping?'.\nDestinations in continuous constructions are expressed in the oblique case, often without explicit postpositions, e.g., 'अपने दफ्तर जा रहा हूँ' meaning 'I am going to my office'.\n"
    }
